====Verb====
{{en-verb|es}}

# {{context|British|obsolete|_|slang|lang=en}} To [[steal]] [[copper]], [[particularly]] from [[ship]] [[hulls]]
#* '''1867''', W. H. Smyth, ''Sailor's Word-book''
#* '''''Toshing''''', a [[cant]] word for stealing copper sheathing from vessels' bottoms, or from dock-yard stores.
# {{context|chiefly|British|uncommon|_|slang|lang=en}} To [[search]] for [[valuables]] in [[sewers]]
#* '''1974''', J. Aiken, ''Midnight is Place vi. 180 You tend to the '''toshing''', let [[mester|Mester]] Hobday tend to the dealing.
# {{context|UK|archaic|_|school|_|slang|lang=en}} To [[use]] a tosh-[[pan]], [[either]] to [[wash]], to [[splash]], or to "[[bath]]"<!--sic-->
#* '''1883''', J.P. Groves, ''From Cadet to Captain'', iii. 227
#*: ‘'''Toshing'''’ was the name given to a punishment inflicted by the cadets on any one of their number who made himself obnoxious. The victim, dressed in full uniform, was forced to run the gauntlet of his brother cadets, who, as he passed, emptied the contents of their ‘tosh-cans’ (small baths holding about three [[gallon]]s of water) over the wretched lad's head.
#* '''1903''', J. S. Farmer &&nbsp;al., ''Slang'', VII. 171/1
#*: He '''toshed''' his [[house beak]] by mistake, and got three hundred.

===Etymology 2===
Compare {{etyl|fro|-}} {{term|tonce|lang=fro||shorn, clipped}} and {{etyl|en|-}} {{term|tonsure|lang=en}}.

====Adjective====
{{en-adj|er}}

# {{context|Scotland|obsolete|lang=en}} [[tight|Tight]].
#* '''1776''', D. Herd, ''Ancient & Modern Scottish Songs''
#*: '''''Tosh''''', tight, neat.
# {{context|Scotland|lang=en}} [[neat|Neat]], [[clean]]; [[tidy]], [[trim]].
#* '''1794''', J. Ritson, ''Scottish Songs'', I. 99
#*: I [[gang]] [[ay]] [[fou]] clean and [[fou]] '''tosh'''
#*: As [[a']] the [[neighbour]]s can tell.
# {{context|Scotland|lang=en}} [[comfortable|Comfortable]], [[agreeable]]; [[friendly]], [[intimate]].
#* '''1821''', ''Blackwood's Edinburgh Magazine'', 10 4
#*: We were a very '''tosh''' and agreeable company.

=====Derived terms=====
* [[toshy]], [[toshly]]

====Adverb====
{{en-adv}}

# {{context|Scotland|lang=en}} [[toshly|Toshly]]: [[neatly]], [[tidily]]
#* '''1808''', J. Mayne, ''Siller Gun'', i. 20
#*: [[shouther|Shouther]] your arms!—O! had them '''tosh''' on, And not [[athraw]]!

====Verb====
{{en-verb|es}}

# {{context|Scotland|lang=en}} To [[make]] ‘tosh’: to [[tidy]], to [[trim]].
#* '''1826 November''', J. Wilson, ''Noctes Ambrosianae'', xxix, in ''Blackwood's Edinburgh Magazine'', 788
#*: [[how|Hoo]] she [[would|wad]] try to '''tosh up'''... her [[breast|breest]].

===Etymology 3===
From 19th-century British slang {{term|tosheroon}}, from or alongside {{term|tusheroon}}, of uncertain derivation from British slang {{term|caroon||[[crown]], ''a 5-[[shilling]] [[silver]] [[coin]]''}}, from {{etyl|pml|en}} and (originally) {{etyl|it|en}} {{term|corona||[[crown]]|lang=it}}. The term was either derived from or influenced by {{term|madza caroon}}, the British slang for the {{etyl|pml|en}} and {{etyl|it|en}} {{term|mezzo||lang=it}} {{term|corona||[[half-crown]]|lang=it}}, possibly under influence from ''[[#Etymology 1|tosh]]'' ("[[copper]] [[item]]s; [[valuables]]") above or from the [[half-crown]]'s value of two [[shilling]]s, [[sixpence]].

====Alternative forms====
* [[tush]]

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~|es}}

# {{context|British|obsolete|_|slang|countable|lang=en}} A [[half-crown]] [[coin]]; its [[value]]
#* '''1933''', {{w|George Orwell}}, ''{{w|Down and Out in Paris and London}}'', xxix
#*: ‘[['ere|’Ere]] [[y'|y’]]are, the best [[rig out|rig-out]] you ever [['ad|’ad]]. A [[tosheroon]] [half a crown]<!--sic--> for the coat, two [['og|’og]]s for the [[trousers]], one and a [[tanner]] for the boots, and a [['og|’og]] for the cap and scarf. That’s seven [[bob]].’
#* '''1961''', Eric Partridge, ''The Routledge Dictionary of Historical Slang''
#*: '''tush''' or '''tosh'''. Money: Cockney: late C.19–20. [[ex|Ex]]: '''[[tusheroon]]'''... But [[w:John Camden Hotten|H.]] errs, I believe: he should mean half-a-crown, for [[tusheroon]] and its C.20 variant [[tossaroon]] (2[[s.]] 6[[d.]]) are manifest corruptions of [[Lingua Franca]] [[madza caroon|MADZA CAROON]].
#: '''1961''', J. Maclaren-Ross, ''Doomsday Book'', i. v. 63
#:: Here's a '''tosh''' to buy yourself some beer.
# {{context|British|obsolete|_|slang|countable|lang=en}} A [[crown]] [[coin]]; its [[value]]
#* '''1859''', {{w|J.C. Hotten}}, ''{{w|A Dictionary of Modern Slang, Cant, and Vulgar Words}}''
#*: ''Half-a-crown'' is known as an {{smallcaps|[[alderman]]}}, {{smallcaps|half a [[bull]]}}, {{smallcaps|half a '''[[tusheroon]]'''}}, and a {{smallcaps|[[madza caroon]]}}; [[whilst]] a ''crown'' piece, or ''five [[shilling]]s'', may be called either a {{smallcaps|[[bull]]}}, or a {{smallcaps|[[caroon]]}}, or a {{smallcaps|[[cartwheel]]}}, or a {{smallcaps|[[coachwheel]]}}, or a {{smallcaps|[[thick-un]]}}, or a '''{{smallcaps|[[tusheroon]]}}'''.
#* '''1912''', J.W. Horsley, ''I Remember'', xii.&nbsp;253
#*: ‘'''Tush'''’, for money, would be an abbreviation of ‘[[tusheroon]]’, which in old [[cant]], and also in [[tinker]] dialect, signified a [[crown]].
# {{context|British|archaic|_|slang|uncountable|lang=en}} Any [[money]], [[particularly]] [[pre-]][[decimalization]] [[British]] [[coinage]]

